#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100759 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e10cdc0-93c4-5b49-8eae-975665bb1d09" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "4d942842-ec76-5ea5-9591-e8b8b8732425" :question/math-academy-id "e-6605" :question/problem "Find the specific solution of the first-order ODE\n\n$$\nxe^y\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}+e^y = e^{x} \\, ,\n$$\n\ngiven that $y(1)=1$ and $x>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's make a substitution\n\n$$\nz=e^y\\,.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ with the chain rule, we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}=e^y\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$ Consequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of  $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}+z=e^x\\,,\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition\n\n$$\nz(1)=e^{y(1)}=e^1=e\\,.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left-hand side of the differential equation is now an exact derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & ={e}^{x}+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+C}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we determine the value of $C$ that satisfies the initial condition $z(1)=e\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z(1) & =e \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1}+C}{1} & =e \\\\ e+C & =e \\\\ C & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have \n\n$$\nz  =   \\frac{e^x}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by substituting the solution into $z=e^y.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y} & =z \\\\ {e}^{y} & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x} \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\  & =x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>0.$ So, the final solution is\n\n$$\ny = x - \\ln (x), \\quad x > 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100760 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8dc2b61-3e0a-5e73-83df-04cc621d5feb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "53e6ba68-a2bc-5761-b32f-4b01057ea925" :question/math-academy-id "e-6606" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the following first-order ODE using the substitution $z=y^3.$\n\n$$\nxy^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = x^2+y^3\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's make a substitution\n\n$$\nz=y^3\\,.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}=3y^2\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\n Consequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)=x^2+z\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3}{x}({x}^{2}+z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =3x+\\frac{3}{x}z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{3}{x}\\,z & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-\\frac{3}{x},\\,Q(x)=3x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-3/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{3}{x}\\,z) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\cdot 3x \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,z & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{z}{{x}^{3}}) & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{z}{{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{z}{{x}^{3}} & =-\\frac{3}{x}+C \\\\ z & =-3{x}^{2}+C{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by substituting the solution into $z=y^3.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3} & =z \\\\ {y}^{3} & =-3{x}^{2}+C{x}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt[3]{-3{x}^{2}+C{x}^{3}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100765 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100934 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e5238ea-c44b-54dd-829b-0da094fb43e6" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a First-Order Linear ODE Using a Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "c906c74a-0478-5b2f-9340-b9bb7c1d2dfd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6867" :question/problem "The substitution $z= y^2$ can be used to transform the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} = \\dfrac{x+2}{2y} + 2x^2y\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form $z'(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2z^{1/2}}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2{z}^{1/2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+2}{2{z}^{1/2}}+2{x}^{2}{z}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x).\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =x+2+4{x}^{2}z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-4{x}^{2}z & =x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x) = - 4x^2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100934 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e5238ea-c44b-54dd-829b-0da094fb43e6" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a First-Order Linear ODE Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04c29e1e-72f5-5778-9190-14096e99274a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281731} {:answer/id #uuid "2ede90c1-9985-5fbb-b71c-2a2ac9b675be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282106} {:answer/id #uuid "4d9b7195-26cd-5b20-b122-b93d7703dc7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282358} {:answer/id #uuid "800de7ca-c36f-55ec-816a-c702b91b521f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282792} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf94ef5-4cf5-5987-a03e-76e204f1250d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccf94ef5-4cf5-5987-a03e-76e204f1250d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283481} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4bbf1a2-d136-5527-93ec-335bba3c14e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5525f3f9-de37-524f-895a-dbf6bcc144ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-166602" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{-3}$ can be used to transform the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-3}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{-1/3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-3{y}^{-4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{3{z}^{4/3}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3{z}^{4/3}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{z}^{4/3}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{{z}^{1/3}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x).\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3z\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3z\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348282 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100934 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e5238ea-c44b-54dd-829b-0da094fb43e6" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a First-Order Linear ODE Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9766a5b4-5490-5eee-b3f1-783a74ac63b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346678} {:answer/id #uuid "c19759f0-6bc3-5195-b4be-cff9483eff83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347024} {:answer/id #uuid "cb13d478-5fe0-5ee0-a40c-fdbff793af9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347125} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cc6ff1-ca27-59e7-9d71-4cf9d0a51b46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347354} {:answer/id #uuid "e57c2fb9-40b2-59fa-bc73-10dac5d1e9b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e57c2fb9-40b2-59fa-bc73-10dac5d1e9b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347371} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d72a2b9-800f-5dd2-b389-9f6fdaf44620" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "528aceec-87ad-5025-88b0-8b8742b0b21b" :question/math-academy-id "q-79781" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{-2}$ can be used to transform the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y(1-x{y}^{2})\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-2{y}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{-3/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{-3/2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{z}^{-1/2}(1-x{z}^{-1}) \\\\ {z}^{-3/2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}{z}^{-1/2}(x{z}^{-1}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x).\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}z(x{z}^{-1}-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}z & ={x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)={x}^{3}.$"}]}
